---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac CFM vs Horsepower",
    "url": "/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Shop Vac vs Dust Extractor",
    "url": "/shop-vac-vs-dust-extractor/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Shop Vac Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Milwaukee vs Makita",
    "url": "/milwaukee-vs-makita/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Tool Brand",
    "url": "/best-cordless-tool-brand/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Cordless Shop Vacs: Battery Wet/Dry Picks for Every Platform";
const description =
  "The best cordless shop vacs for Milwaukee, Ryobi, RIDGID, Makita, DeWalt and Craftsman batteries, compared on rated airflow, hose size, tank and runtime.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-m18-fuel-6-gal-0910",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 6 Gallon Wet/Dry Vacuum (0910-20)",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "M18, 6 Gallon, Brushless, 1-7/8 in Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The M18 FUEL 6 Gallon is the cordless shop vac that comes closest to replacing a mid-size corded unit, and it is our pick if you already run Milwaukee batteries. Retail listings for the 0910-20 consistently give 87 CFM of airflow, a 6 gallon tank, and a 1-7/8 inch by 9 foot hose, which is the hose size we prefer for general garage and jobsite debris because it bridges and clogs far less often than the 1-1/4 inch hoses on compact vacs. Milwaukee says the brushless POWERSTATE motor is designed to rival 3.5 peak HP corded wet/dry vacs, and quotes up to 31 minutes of continuous run time in Max Power mode on an M18 HIGH OUTPUT HD12.0 pack. That runtime figure is the key caveat: it assumes the biggest battery Milwaukee sells, and a common 5.0Ah pack will give you a fraction of it. One retailer lists 60 inches of sealed suction against the 87 CFM figure while another reverses the two numbers, so treat the suction figure as unconfirmed until you see Milwaukee's own spec sheet. As a bare tool it is expensive, but the hose size, tank size and airflow put it in a different class from the 2 gallon cordless vacs.",
    "pros": [
      "Retailers list 87 CFM, the highest airflow of any 18V vac here",
      "1-7/8 in x 9 ft hose handles chunky debris without clogging",
      "6 gallon tank is genuinely useful for wet pickup",
      "Milwaukee quotes up to 31 minutes in Max Power mode on an HD12.0 pack"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rated runtime assumes Milwaukee's largest battery",
      "Bare tool price is high before you add batteries",
      "Retailers disagree on the sealed suction figure"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you own M18 batteries and want one cordless vac that handles real garage and jobsite cleanup, the M18 FUEL 6 Gallon is the one to buy, as long as you have a high-capacity pack to run it."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-18v-one-plus-6-gal-pcl735",
    "name": "Ryobi 18V ONE+ 6 Gallon Wet/Dry Vac (PCL735B)",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "18V ONE+, 6 Gallon, 2-Speed, 1-7/8 in Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The PCL735 gives you most of what the Milwaukee offers at a much lower price, which makes it the default recommendation for homeowners on the Ryobi ONE+ platform. Ryobi lists up to 80 CFM of airflow, up to 36 inches of sealed suction, a 6 gallon tank and a 7 foot by 1-7/8 inch hose, and it carries a 3-year limited warranty. The two-speed motor matters on a battery vac: the low setting trades some suction for noticeably longer runtime, which is what you want for dust and light debris, while the high setting is for wet pickup and heavier material. Ryobi lists the sound level as low as 66.1 dB(A) on its quieter setting. A review lists it at around $149 bare and $199 as a kit with a 4Ah battery and charger, though prices move and you should check the current listing. Weight figures conflict between Ryobi's US and Canadian pages, at 16 and 19.4 pounds, so do not rely on either if you need to carry it up stairs often. Be careful with the model number: the PCL734 is a smaller 3 gallon Ryobi Link vac, not this one.",
    "pros": [
      "Ryobi lists up to 80 CFM and 36 in of sealed suction",
      "Full-size 1-7/8 in hose on a 6 gallon tank",
      "Two-speed motor stretches runtime on light work",
      "3-year limited warranty and a low bare-tool price"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Ryobi's own pages disagree on weight",
      "Easy to confuse with the smaller PCL734",
      "High speed drains a 4Ah pack quickly"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a Ryobi household, the PCL735 is the cordless shop vac to buy: full-size hose, 6 gallon tank and 80 CFM rated airflow for roughly half the price of the Milwaukee."
  },
  {
    "id": "ridgid-18v-9-gal-nxt-hd0919",
    "name": "RIDGID 18V 9 Gallon Cordless NXT Wet/Dry Vac (HD0919)",
    "badge": "Biggest Tank",
    "category": "18V, 9 Gallon, One or Two Batteries, 1-7/8 in Hose",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "RIDGID's 9 gallon cordless NXT is the one to look at if tank capacity is the reason you want a shop vac, for example pulling water out of a flooded floor drain or emptying a window well. Retail listings describe it as a 9 gallon, 18V cordless NXT wet/dry vac that runs on one battery but accepts two for longer runtime, and that works with any RIDGID 18V battery. It ships with a 1-7/8 inch Tug-A-Long, Dual-Flex locking hose, three extension wands and car, utility and wet nozzles. Home Depot lists the airflow at 82 CFM, and a Pro Tool Reviews write-up notes that a water lift figure is not published, so you cannot compare its sealed suction with the Ryobi or Milwaukee. The second battery bay is the practical advantage: on a vac this size, a single pack runs out long before the drum fills. RIDGID's lifetime service agreement applies to many of its 18V tools when registered, but terms vary by product, so check that the vac qualifies before counting on it.",
    "pros": [
      "9 gallon drum, the largest tank in this group",
      "Accepts two batteries for longer runtime",
      "Home Depot lists 82 CFM airflow",
      "Locking 1-7/8 in hose and a full nozzle kit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No published water lift figure",
      "Bulky to carry once full of water",
      "RIDGID batteries are less widely used than Milwaukee or DeWalt"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the RIDGID 9 Gallon if you want the biggest drum on a battery vac and can feed it two packs, especially for wet cleanup."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xgt-gcv03-4-gal",
    "name": "Makita 40V max XGT 4 Gallon Wet/Dry Dust Extractor/Vacuum (GCV03Z)",
    "badge": "Most Powerful",
    "category": "40V XGT, 4 Gallon, Two Battery Bays",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Makita's XGT vacuums are rated well above the 18V machines, and the GCV03Z is the one to consider if raw suction matters more than cost. Makita's listing, as reported by Pro Tool Reviews, gives 148 CFM and 92 inches of water lift, with a two-bay battery system that Makita says delivers up to twice the run time. It comes with a telescoping wand, an 11 inch floor nozzle, a cloth filter and ten disposal bags, and Pro Tool Reviews lists the noise at 67 decibels. These are manufacturer figures and we have not measured them, but even allowing for optimistic ratings, a 40V platform has more headroom than an 18V motor. The catch is price and platform: one retailer listed the bare tool at about $843, and XGT batteries are not compatible with Makita's 18V LXT tools. A smaller GCV01Z with a 2.1 gallon tank carries the same rating at a lower price if you do not need the capacity.",
    "pros": [
      "Makita rates it at 148 CFM and 92 in of water lift",
      "Two battery bays for longer runtime",
      "Bags and cloth filter suit dust extraction work",
      "Rated noise of 67 dB per Pro Tool Reviews"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Very expensive as a bare tool",
      "XGT batteries do not fit 18V LXT tools",
      "Only worth it if you already run XGT"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you are already on Makita XGT and need serious suction without a cord, the GCV03Z is the most powerful cordless vac here on paper, at a price that only makes sense for professional use."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xcv11z-18v-2-gal-hepa",
    "name": "Makita 18V LXT 2 Gallon HEPA Wet/Dry Vacuum (XCV11Z)",
    "badge": "Best Compact HEPA",
    "category": "18V LXT, 2 Gallon, HEPA, Brushless",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The XCV11Z is a compact, brushless vac with a HEPA filter, which makes it a good match for drywall dust, sanding and work inside occupied rooms. Retailers list it at 57 CFM with 27 inches of water lift, and Makita quotes up to 60 minutes of continuous run time on a 5.0Ah battery. Makita lists the weight at 10.1 pounds, and one retailer gives a working sound level of 76 dB(A). The HEPA filter is rated to capture 99.97 percent of particulates at 0.3 microns, which matters because a standard cartridge filter on many shop vacs passes the finest dust straight back into the room. The trade-off is the 2 gallon tank and a smaller hose, so this is a tool for cleaning up after a job rather than clearing a flooded garage. Prices vary by retailer, roughly $220 to $250 for the bare tool in the listings we found.",
    "pros": [
      "HEPA filter rated at 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns",
      "Retailers list 57 CFM and 27 in of water lift",
      "Makita quotes up to 60 minutes on a 5.0Ah pack",
      "Light at a listed 10.1 lb"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "2 gallon tank fills quickly on wet work",
      "Not suited to bulky debris",
      "Pricier than other compact vacs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For an 18V LXT owner who needs clean dust collection indoors, the XCV11Z is the compact cordless vac to buy, provided you accept a small tank."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcv581h-20v-max",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX Cordless/Corded Wet-Dry Vacuum (DCV581H)",
    "badge": "Best Cordless or Corded",
    "category": "20V MAX or 120V, 2 Gallon, Washable Filter",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The DCV581H earns its place because it runs on a 20V MAX battery or plugs into a wall outlet, so a dead battery never stops the job. It has a 2 gallon tank, and listings give 31 CFM of airflow with a 5 foot by 1-1/4 inch crush-resistant hose that stores on the unit. The filter is washable and reusable; listings disagree on whether it is rated at 99.97 percent efficiency at 0.3 microns or at 1 micron, so do not assume true HEPA performance without checking DeWalt's documentation. It also works as a blower for clearing a work area. With 31 CFM it has the lowest rated airflow here, so treat it as a compact cleanup vac for job sites and vehicles, not a replacement for a full-size unit. Ace Hardware lists it at $219.99 and another retailer at $199, tool only.",
    "pros": [
      "Runs on battery or 120V power",
      "Hose stores on the unit, easy to carry",
      "Washable, reusable filter",
      "Doubles as a blower"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Lowest rated airflow here at 31 CFM",
      "Short 5 ft hose",
      "Filter rating is inconsistent across listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For DeWalt users who want a compact vac that never runs out of power, the DCV581H is the pick, as long as you do not expect full-size shop vac suction."
  },
  {
    "id": "craftsman-v20-cmcv002b",
    "name": "CRAFTSMAN V20 2 Gallon Cordless Wet/Dry Vac (CMCV002B)",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "category": "V20, 2 Gallon, HEPA-Rated Filter",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The CMCV002B is the inexpensive way to add a cordless vac if you already own CRAFTSMAN V20 batteries. Craftsman and retailers list a 2 gallon tank, 35 CFM of suction and a 7 foot 8 inch hose at 1-1/4 inch diameter, with a wide nozzle and crevice tool included. The filter is described as HEPA rated, trapping 99.7 percent of dust and allergens, which is a lower figure than the 99.97 percent true HEPA standard, so read it as a good fine-dust filter rather than a certified HEPA one. Listings disagree on weight, from about 7.2 to 9.25 pounds, and on dimensions. This is a car, workshop-bench and quick-spill vac; the small hose and tank make it a poor choice for drywall cleanup at volume or for water extraction.",
    "pros": [
      "Low cost if you own V20 batteries",
      "Listed at 35 CFM, more than the DeWalt",
      "Long 7 ft 8 in hose for a compact vac",
      "Filter rated at 99.7 percent"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Filter is not true HEPA by the usual standard",
      "Small tank and 1-1/4 in hose",
      "Listings disagree on weight and size"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you are on CRAFTSMAN V20 and want a cheap cordless vac for cars and quick cleanups, the CMCV002B does the job without pretending to be a full shop vac."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cordless Shop Vacs",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-cordless-shop-vac/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best cordless shop vac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you own Milwaukee M18 batteries, the M18 FUEL 6 Gallon is the strongest 18V option here, with retailers listing 87 CFM and a 1-7/8 inch hose. Ryobi ONE+ owners should buy the PCL735, which Ryobi rates at up to 80 CFM for a much lower price. On other platforms, pick the best vac that runs on batteries you already have."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cordless shop vacs as powerful as corded ones?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The larger 18V models get close to mid-size corded vacs on rated airflow, and Makita's 40V XGT vac is rated higher still. The difference is runtime: a corded vac runs indefinitely, while a battery vac runs until the pack is empty. For long jobs and heavy water extraction, corded still wins."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a cordless shop vac run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the battery. Milwaukee quotes up to 31 minutes for its M18 FUEL 6 Gallon on a 12Ah pack, and Makita quotes up to 60 minutes for the XCV11Z on a 5.0Ah pack. Smaller packs scale roughly in proportion, and high power settings and cold temperatures cut runtime further."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a cordless shop vac pick up water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, all of the vacs here are wet/dry models, but tank size decides how useful they are. A 2 gallon tank fills quickly; the 6 and 9 gallon units are much better for spills, leaks and flooded drains. Always switch to the wet-pickup filter setup the manual specifies before vacuuming water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size cordless shop vac do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For cars, benches and quick dust cleanup, a 2 gallon compact vac is easier to carry and use. For garage debris and water, choose a 6 or 9 gallon model with a 1-7/8 inch hose. Our guide to what size shop vac you need goes through tank size by job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do cordless shop vacs come with batteries?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most are sold as bare tools, with batteries and a charger sold separately, which is why buying on your existing platform makes sense. Some brands sell kit versions with a battery and charger, such as Ryobi's PCL735K, which a review lists with a 4Ah pack."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a cordless shop vac for drywall dust?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if it has a filter rated for fine dust. A true HEPA filter, like the one Makita rates on the XCV11Z, captures the fine fraction that a standard cartridge passes through. For regular sanding or concrete cutting, a dedicated dust extractor is the better tool."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best cordless shop vac",
      "battery shop vac",
      "cordless wet dry vac",
      "battery powered shop vac",
      "cordless shop vacuum"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Battery Picks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Cordless <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Shop Vacs</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          One strong battery wet/dry vac for each major platform, compared on rated airflow, hose size, tank and the runtime you will actually get.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-m18-fuel-6-gal-0910"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#cordless-vs-corded" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Cordless vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#airflow" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. CFM and Suction</a>
          <a href="#runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Runtime</a>
          <a href="#capacity" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Tank Size</a>
          <a href="#hose-filter" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Hose and Filters</a>
          <a href="#platform" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Platform</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Care and Storage</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best cordless shop vac is the one that runs on batteries you already own. After that, look at airflow, hose diameter and tank size, and read every runtime figure with the battery it assumes, because that is where battery vacs disappoint.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications and retailer listings, not from a lab test. Every airflow, suction and runtime figure below is attributed to Makita, Milwaukee, Ryobi, RIDGID, DeWalt or Craftsman, or to the retailer that published it, and where two sources disagree we say so rather than picking the more flattering number. That matters more for cordless vacs than for most tools, because battery vac specs are often quoted at the highest power setting on the largest battery the brand sells.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started from one rule: the right cordless shop vac is almost always the one that runs on batteries you already own. A battery vac on a platform you do not use is a corded vac with a much higher price, because you have to buy packs and a charger before it does anything. So the list covers one strong option on each major platform rather than ranking seven machines from the same brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within each platform we looked at four things in order. Airflow in CFM, because that decides whether debris actually travels up the hose. Hose diameter, because a 1-1/4 inch hose clogs on wood chips and leaves no matter how strong the motor is. Tank size, because a 2 gallon tank is a cleanup tool and a 6 or 9 gallon tank is a water-extraction tool. And the filter, because a vac that blows fine dust back into the room is worse than no vac for drywall and sanding work.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also checked what each brand says about runtime, and which battery that figure assumes. A runtime quoted on a 12Ah pack is not the runtime you get on the 4Ah pack that came in your drill kit, and we flag that wherever it applies. If you are still deciding whether you need a battery vac at all, our main <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac guide</a> covers the corded machines that still win on raw power per dollar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless-vs-corded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Can a Cordless Shop Vac Replace a Corded One?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, not entirely, and it helps to be honest about that before spending several hundred dollars. A corded shop vac runs as long as you want, and a mid-size corded unit is cheap. A cordless vac runs as long as the battery lasts, and a large-capacity pack can cost as much as the vac itself. The question is not which one is more powerful but which jobs you actually do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless wins where the cord is the problem. Cleaning out a car in the driveway, vacuuming sawdust at the end of a job on a site with no power yet, chasing a spill in the basement, or tidying a workbench in the time it takes to find an extension cord. These are short, frequent jobs, and the vac that is always in reach gets used. The existing pick on our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac roundup</a> makes the same point: owners use the cordless vac far more often simply because there is no cord to deal with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded still wins for long jobs and heavy material. Emptying a flooded floor, clearing a garage full of leaves, or running a vac as dust extraction behind a sander for an hour will drain any battery quickly. If your main use is one of those, buy a corded vac first and a compact cordless one second, or choose a battery vac that takes two packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle path. DeWalt's DCV581H runs on battery or a wall outlet, and RIDGID's and Makita's two-bay designs double the battery capacity you can carry. If you want one machine to do most things, those designs remove the biggest weakness of a battery vac.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for your battery platform first, specs second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A cordless vac on a platform you do not own costs the vac plus batteries plus a charger. A good vac on your existing platform will almost always be the better buy than a slightly better vac on someone else's.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

