        <div id="airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CFM, Water Lift and Why Peak HP Disappears
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corded shop vacs are still sold on peak horsepower, a figure that describes the electrical draw when the motor stalls and has little to do with cleaning. Cordless vacs mostly skip it, which is a small mercy. What you will see instead are two numbers: airflow in cubic feet per minute, and sealed suction, often called water lift and measured in inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Airflow is how much air the vac moves with an open hose, and it decides whether loose debris travels up the hose and into the drum. Water lift is how hard the vac pulls against a sealed opening, and it decides whether the vac can lift water, pull debris out of carpet or pull through a clogged filter. Our guide to <a href="/shop-vac-cfm-vs-horsepower/">CFM versus horsepower</a> explains the physics in more detail, but the short version is that you want both, and for general garage cleanup airflow matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the listed numbers, the spread here is wide. Makita's 40V XGT vac is rated at 148 CFM. The 18V Milwaukee and Ryobi 6 gallon units are listed at 87 and 80 CFM, and RIDGID's 9 gallon at 82 CFM. The compact 2 gallon machines sit between 31 and 57 CFM. Those are manufacturer and retailer figures, not measurements, and brands do not all test the same way, so read small differences as noise and large ones as real.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary when a listing swaps the two numbers. We found one retailer listing the Milwaukee at 60 CFM and 87 inches of water lift while others list 87 CFM and 60 inches of suction, and a Milwaukee 2 gallon listing that gave the water lift figure as the CFM. When a spec looks odd, check the brand's own page before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Runtime and Battery Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Runtime is the number that disappoints cordless vac buyers most, and it is almost always because the published figure assumes a battery you do not have. Milwaukee quotes up to 31 minutes for the M18 FUEL 6 Gallon in Max Power mode on an HD12.0 pack. Makita quotes up to 60 minutes for the XCV11Z on a 5.0Ah pack. Milwaukee quotes 30 or more minutes for its M18 2 gallon vac on a 9.0Ah pack. Those are useful figures, but they are best cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple way to estimate your own runtime is to scale by amp-hours. If a vac is rated for 31 minutes on a 12Ah pack, a 6Ah pack on the same platform will give you roughly half that, and a 4Ah pack roughly a third. The real number will be a little worse, because smaller packs sag more under heavy load and many vacs run the motor harder than a drill does. Cold garages also cut lithium-ion capacity, which matters if you plan to vacuum snow melt off a winter floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical fixes help more than chasing a bigger battery. First, use the low setting where the vac has one. Ryobi's two-speed motor and Makita's variable power dial exist precisely to trade suction for runtime on dust and light debris. Second, consider a two-bay vac. The RIDGID 9 gallon and Makita XGT units both take a second battery, which roughly doubles the energy available without buying a giant pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you rely on your batteries for other tools, think about the knock-on effect. A vac that drains your only high-capacity pack will leave your circular saw idle. Our explainer on <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/">cordless tool batteries</a> covers how amp-hours, voltage and high-output cells affect what you can run.
          </p>

