        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tank Size: Smaller Than You Think
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tank size on a cordless vac is less important than it looks, because the battery usually runs out before the drum fills with dry debris. A 2 gallon tank holds a surprising amount of sawdust and the contents of a car interior. Where tank size really matters is water. A gallon of water weighs a little over eight pounds, so a full 6 gallon drum is heavy, and a full 9 gallon drum is a two-hand lift.
          </p>

          <p>
            For dry cleanup, a compact 2 gallon vac like the Makita XCV11Z or the CRAFTSMAN CMCV002B is easier to carry up a ladder, into a car or across a job site, and you will use it more often because of that. For wet cleanup, such as a leaking water heater or a basement drain backing up, the 6 and 9 gallon units are the ones that actually help, because you are not stopping every few minutes to empty a small tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check how the vac empties. A drum you can tip into a floor drain or out a door is fine for water; a small tank with a tight lid is fiddly. If water is the main job, also check whether the vac uses a foam sleeve or needs the dry filter removed for wet pickup, because running a paper filter wet ruins it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure how big a vac you need at all, our guide to <a href="/what-size-shop-vac-do-i-need/">what size shop vac you need</a> walks through tank size by job. For most battery vac buyers, the answer is the smallest tank that handles your wettest likely job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hose-filter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hose Diameter and Filters
          </h2>

          <p>
            The hose is the most underrated spec on any shop vac. A 1-7/8 inch hose passes wood chips, leaves and drywall scraps that will bridge and clog in a 1-1/4 inch hose almost immediately. The Milwaukee, Ryobi and RIDGID large vacs all use 1-7/8 inch hoses, which is a big part of why they are the ones to buy for garage and yard debris. The compact Makita, DeWalt and Craftsman units use smaller hoses, which concentrate suction well for fine dust and connect more easily to power tool dust ports, but they are poor at bulky material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose length matters too. A 9 foot hose on the Milwaukee lets you leave the vac on the floor and reach a workbench; the 5 foot hose on the DeWalt means carrying the vac with you. Neither is wrong, but think about how you will actually use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filters decide what happens to fine dust. A standard cartridge filter catches visible debris but passes the finest particles straight back into the air, which is exactly the dust that matters for drywall, sanding and concrete work. A true HEPA filter is rated to capture 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns. Makita rates the XCV11Z's filter to that standard. Craftsman describes its filter as HEPA rated at 99.7 percent, and DeWalt listings disagree on whether its filter is rated at 0.3 microns or 1 micron. Read the actual rating, not the word HEPA on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are cutting or grinding concrete, or doing regular sanding indoors, a cordless vac with a proper HEPA filter is a step up, but a dedicated dust extractor is the better tool. Our comparison of a <a href="/shop-vac-vs-dust-extractor/">shop vac versus a dust extractor</a> explains where the line falls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Pick the Platform You Already Own
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the battery is the expensive part, the platform decision usually makes itself. If you own Milwaukee M18, look at the M18 FUEL 6 Gallon first, or Milwaukee's M18 2 gallon if you only need a compact vac. If you own Ryobi ONE+, the PCL735 is the obvious pick and the cheapest full-size cordless vac here. RIDGID 18V owners get the biggest tank. DeWalt 20V MAX owners get the battery-or-corded DCV581H. Makita owners choose between the 18V LXT XCV11Z and the much more powerful 40V XGT machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not own a platform yet, a cordless shop vac is a poor reason to choose one. The vac is a supporting tool, and the drill, impact driver and saws you buy will define which platform suits you. Our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">best cordless tool brand</a> guide covers that decision, and our head-to-heads such as <a href="/milwaukee-vs-makita/">Milwaukee vs Makita</a>, <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> and <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a> go platform by platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita deserves a specific warning. Its 18V LXT and 40V XGT batteries are not interchangeable, so the XGT vac only makes sense if you already run XGT tools. Makita does sell an adaptor that lets XGT chargers charge LXT packs, but that does not let LXT batteries power XGT tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid third-party battery adapters that let one brand's packs run another brand's vac. They bypass the communication between the pack and the tool that protects both from overheating and over-discharge, and every major brand says they void the warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Care, Storage and Making Batteries Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cordless vac lasts longer with a little care, and most of it is about the filter and the battery. Tap the filter clean after dusty jobs, because a loaded filter chokes airflow long before the tank is full and makes the motor work harder, which drains the battery faster. On HEPA filters, follow the maker's cleaning guidance; some are washable and some are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never put away a vac with water in the tank. Empty it, wipe it out and leave the lid off to dry, or you will open it next month to a smell that does not come out. If you vacuum water, make sure the right filter or foam sleeve is fitted before you start, because a wet paper filter is ruined and can let water reach the motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look after the batteries. Lithium-ion packs prefer to be stored partly charged in a cool, dry place, not left on the charger in a hot garage all summer or in a freezing shed all winter. A pack that is too cold may also refuse to charge until it warms up, which is a protection feature, not a fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check warranty terms before you need them. Ryobi lists a 3-year limited warranty on the PCL735. Milwaukee, Makita, DeWalt, RIDGID and Craftsman each have their own tool and battery terms, and some require registration. Keeping the receipt and registering the vac takes five minutes and turns a failure into a warranty claim.
          </p>
        </div>

