          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you own Milwaukee M18 batteries, the M18 FUEL 6 Gallon is the strongest 18V option here, with retailers listing 87 CFM and a 1-7/8 inch hose. Ryobi ONE+ owners should buy the PCL735, which Ryobi rates at up to 80 CFM for a much lower price. On other platforms, pick the best vac that runs on batteries you already have.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless shop vacs as powerful as corded ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The larger 18V models get close to mid-size corded vacs on rated airflow, and Makita's 40V XGT vac is rated higher still. The difference is runtime: a corded vac runs indefinitely, while a battery vac runs until the pack is empty. For long jobs and heavy water extraction, corded still wins.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a cordless shop vac run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the battery. Milwaukee quotes up to 31 minutes for its M18 FUEL 6 Gallon on a 12Ah pack, and Makita quotes up to 60 minutes for the XCV11Z on a 5.0Ah pack. Smaller packs scale roughly in proportion, and high power settings and cold temperatures cut runtime further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless shop vac pick up water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, all of the vacs here are wet/dry models, but tank size decides how useful they are. A 2 gallon tank fills quickly; the 6 and 9 gallon units are much better for spills, leaks and flooded drains. Always switch to the wet-pickup filter setup the manual specifies before vacuuming water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size cordless shop vac do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cars, benches and quick dust cleanup, a 2 gallon compact vac is easier to carry and use. For garage debris and water, choose a 6 or 9 gallon model with a 1-7/8 inch hose. Our guide to what size shop vac you need goes through tank size by job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cordless shop vacs come with batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are sold as bare tools, with batteries and a charger sold separately, which is why buying on your existing platform makes sense. Some brands sell kit versions with a battery and charger, such as Ryobi's PCL735K, which a review lists with a 4Ah pack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a cordless shop vac for drywall dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it has a filter rated for fine dust. A true HEPA filter, like the one Makita rates on the XCV11Z, captures the fine fraction that a standard cartridge passes through. For regular sanding or concrete cutting, a dedicated dust extractor is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>

